'''''Geranium rotundifolium''''' ('''round-leaved crane's-bill'''), is a species of annual herb in the family [[Geraniaceae]]. It is native in temperate climates across much of [[Europe]], northern [[Africa]], and southwestern [[Asia]]; its distribution is spreading north, in response to [[global warming]].<ref name=BSBI_Atlas2020>{{cite web |title=Round-leaved Crane's-bill ''Geranium rotundifolium'' L. |work=BSBI Online Plant Atlas 2020 |editor1=P.A. Stroh |editor2=T. A. Humphrey |editor3=R.J. Burkmar |editor4=O.L. Pescott |editor5=D.B. Roy |editor6=K.J. Walker |publisher=Botanical Society of Britain and Ireland |date=2020 |url=https://plantatlas2020.org/atlas/2cd4p9h.14d |access-date=21 June 2024 }}</ref><ref name = "Streeter">Streeter D, Hart-Davies C, Hardcastle A, Cole F, Harper L. 2009. ''Collins Flower Guide''. Harper Collins {{ISBN|9-78-000718389-0}}</ref><ref name="POWO">{{Cite POWO |last1=POWO |author-link1=Plants of the World Online |id=373602-1 |title=''Geranium rotundifolium'' L. |access-date=21 June 2024 }}</ref> The species favours dry, sandy or stony habitat, including old walls, rail ballast, and building rubble, including in urban areas.<ref name=BSBI_Atlas2020 /> 

The basal leaves are simple, 3–6 cm across, broad rounded kidney-shaped, with a shallowly lobed margin; leaves higher up the stem are smaller and more deeply lobed.<ref name = "Streeter" /> The flowering stems can grow to 20&nbsp;cm tall; both the stems and leaves are hairy with glandular hairs.<ref name = "Streeter" /><ref>{{Cite web|title=Geranium rotundifolium L. - Encyclopedia of Life|url=https://eol.org/pages/346199|access-date=2022-02-04|website=eol.org}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Geranium rotundifolium |url=https://www.maltawildplants.com/GERN/Geranium_rotundifolium.php |website=Malta Wild Plants}}</ref> The [[flower]]s are small, 10–16 mm diameter, pink, with five petals with a rounded to shallowly two-lobed apex (unlike similar related species, which have a more deeply notched petal apex<ref name = "Streeter" />). The flowers are visited by sweat bees, small carpenter bees, myopa, and cabbage butterfly.

